Ladybird runs the opaque-response-safelist check of the Fetch draft on
each opaque response at fetch response handover: header-based allowing
and blocking, then image and audio/video sniffing of the first bytes,
and in place of the draft’s full JavaScript parse, a sniff that blocks
only HTML, XML, JSON objects, and script-breaking prefixes.

Chrome’s ORB (OpaqueResponseBlockingAnalyzer, in services/network/
public/cpp/orb/orb_impl.cc) also stops short of the full parse: It
sniffs for HTML, XML, JSON, and parser-breaker prefixes in
orb_sniffers.cc, and takes image formats its sniffer doesn’t recognize
as safelisted (IsNonSniffableImageMimeType). Firefox implements the
draft in netwerk/protocol/http/OpaqueResponseUtils.cpp, with
GetOpaqueResponseBlockedReason and OpaqueResponseFilter, and validates
the remaining responses as JavaScript. WebKit has no ORB at all.
